<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    A long-term care pharmacy serving nursing homes, assisted living and group homes is a dispenser under
    DSCSA. Three questions come up more than any others.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Is delivering to a facility a DSCSA transaction?</h2>
    <p>
      DSCSA&rsquo;s definition of a transaction excludes dispensing a product pursuant to a prescription, and the
      dispenser rules for passing on transaction records say &ldquo;not including dispensing to a patient.&rdquo;
      Filling residents&rsquo; prescriptions and delivering them isn&rsquo;t a trading-partner transfer, so it
      doesn&rsquo;t carry DSCSA transaction information. Your delivery records still matter for state rules,
      facility contracts and cold chain; see <%= link_to "DSCSA and pharmacy delivery", guide_path("dscsa-pharmacy-delivery") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Selling to another pharmacy</h2>
    <p>
      When one pharmacy sells a product to another to fill a prescription for an identified patient, the selling
      pharmacy isn&rsquo;t required to provide transaction information or a transaction statement. Transfers to
      replenish the other pharmacy&rsquo;s stock don&rsquo;t qualify. That&rsquo;s the &ldquo;specific patient
      need&rdquo; provision; if you do this often, document the patient need each time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The headcount question for multi-site groups</h2>
    <%= render "guides/qualify" %>
    <p>
      LTC pharmacy groups often run several sites. The count covers the whole owning company, not one location,
      so a group can be over 25 even when each site is small. If you&rsquo;re over, the enhanced requirements
      already apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What applies today either way</h2>
    <%= render "guides/applies_today" %>
  </section>
<% end %>
